{
  "schema_version": "1.7.4",
  "published": "2026-10-29T13:37:38Z",
  "modified": "2026-10-29T13:37:38Z",
  "summary": "numbors ships entire CLI as heavily obfuscated bundles",
  "details": "The numbors npm package publishes its entire shipped surface as heavily obfuscated JavaScript: every file under dist/ (the main entry dist/mcp.js, dist/config.js, dist/format.js, dist/command-catalog.js, dist/surface-parity.js, and ~90 command modules under dist/commands/) is minified with hex-identifier obfuscation (_0x<hex> variable names) rather than readable source. The package appears to be a CLI/MCP tool covering accounting, CRM, payroll, POS, and billing operations against a backend service. No concrete installer-harm fingerprint is visible from the shipped artifact — no lifecycle scripts fetching remote payloads, no identified credential-store reads, no hardcoded attacker C2, and no identified exfiltration path were observed — but the uniform obfuscation across every shipped file prevents semantic verification of what the CLI does when invoked, including how it handles any API tokens or financial data a user provides.",
